MAIL -- the mailbox directory, and two programs that disagree about it=====================================================================Two programs here keep mail in this directory and they do not use the samefilename.`mail' -- the UUCP mail reader and sender -- writes the mailbox as the bareLOGIN NAME.  `mail tester' takes a message from the keyboard, a line holdingone dot ending it, and files it as MAIL/tester; `mail' on its own opens whatis waiting there and prompts, one message at a time.  It also wants a scratchdevice at /r0: a real OS-9 system has a RAM disk there, and under os9exec`mount -r=256k /r0' makes one.  DOC/README-RUNNING lists the twenty programsthat want it.`read_mail' -- elvis and vi's mail-reading helper -- opens MAIL/mail_<user>instead, and is invoked BY the editor rather than from a command line: itquotes what it finds so you can reply to it.`mail_' is here and EMPTY.  Without the file read_mail could not openanything and exited without a word, which is why it sat in the "silent for noknown reason" list for a long time.  With an empty mailbox it does exactlywhat it should: it reads to end-of-file and has nothing to quote.This directory ships with the collection, so neither program has to makeit.A THIRD CONVENTION, and it is the one that catches people out.  `rmail'and `mailx' -- the UUCP delivery program and the ELM mail reader -- wantthe mailbox to be a DIRECTORY named for the user, not a file.  Runeither and it says so in its own words:    rmail: dolocal: can't change to mailbox:           /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester/tester...error #214    mailx: can't change to your mailbox directory: 'su'...error 214Note what mailx asks for: it looks the user up by NUMBER, and everythinghere runs as 0.0, whose first entry in SYS/password is `su'.  Neither isbroken and neither is given what it wants; the folder they are pointedat is a plain file, which is what `mail', `messages' and `frm' expect.Measured 2026-09-21.